(FIL-70: `is available` works on a string literal, a text variable and a buffer, and is not limited to plain files - a directory and a device node both answer true.
 Also covers: FIL-71, FIL-72, FIL-73.
 The access(2)/F_OK claim itself is not directly observable from Vox; what
 is observable is that every path KIND the manual names answers, which is
 what this probe pins down.
 Run from the vox-fuzz worktree root; the probe creates everything it
 needs under vf_scratch/files/ and touches nothing else.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox docs/ledger/probes/files/FIL-70.vox -o p && ./p
 actual output:
   File found.
   a text variable works as a path expression
   a buffer works as a path expression
   a directory is available, so the check is not limited to plain files
   a device node is available
)
Create a directory called "vf_scratch".
Create a directory called "vf_scratch/files".
open a file for writing called seedfile at "vf_scratch/files/present.txt".
Write "here" to seedfile.
Close seedfile.
If "vf_scratch/files/present.txt" is available then,
    print "File found.".
a text called 'the path' is "vf_scratch/files/present.txt".
If 'the path' is available then,
    print "a text variable works as a path expression".
a buffer called 'the path buffer' is "vf_scratch/files/present.txt".
If 'the path buffer' is available then,
    print "a buffer works as a path expression".
If "vf_scratch/files" is available then,
    print "a directory is available, so the check is not limited to plain files".
If "/dev/null" is available then,
    print "a device node is available".
